A||||42||merlin||Is the Fiero an ‘old man’s’ car?…||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||09-06-2000||09:41 AM||||I heard someplace that when the Fiero was originally introduced it was primarily targeted at the twenty-something market, but now the majority of Fiero owners are thirty-something and forty-something.  I’m 31, so this statement would be correct for me, but is this true for the majority of the members of this forum?  What are some of the age groups out there?  If you believe this statement is valid, are you a Fiero owner because you admired them during the eighties but couldn’t shell out the cash for one then, or has the Fiero become a recent interest?  For myself, I only recently became interested in the Fiero when I realized that they are much, much more than a kit car donor.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||09-06-2000||09:48 AM||||<BR>I have liked Fieros since 88, I was 16/17 years old then, and didn't have the finances or parental concent(sp) to purchase one.<P>I am now 28 and purchased my first real (read running) Fiero at 27.<P>So no, it is not an "old man's" car.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||09-06-2000||10:00 AM||||I bought my first on in 1986... Black GT automatic. I was 28. I bought my next street-able one, a Bubble Gim Blue 1987 GT this year... I'm 42. I think the car is mostly attractive to people who were old enough to appreciate the car when it was new or nearly new. But... WHO CARES! I like them, that's all that matters.<P>I have a neighbor with a 16 year old son. He is saving for a Honda to rice out. He was over at my house last week and was really admiring my GT. He didn't even know what it was until I told him and he was really amazed that the engine was in the back. After a breaf show-in-tell, he made the comment... "maybe I'll buy a Fiero instead of a Honda". It aint rice, but it can still turn the head of the yonger generation.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
